# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S12 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic has "actual_desire_general → general_desirable" and "established → actual_desire_general"; sidgwick.logic.2 and sidgwick.logic.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the general happiness is desirable, i.e. what each individual ought to desire or aim at realising in action" and "Mill's reasoning establishes that the general happiness is desirable" are true, which the second allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/answered: S12: answered no, which keeps sidgwick.logic, to: Suppose "the general happiness is desirable, i.e. what each individual ought to desire or aim at realising in action" and "Mill's reasoning establishes that the general happiness is desirable" are true and "there is an actual desire for the general happiness" is false. Is S12, "But this proposition is not established by Mill’s reasoning, even if we grant that what is actually desired may be legitimately inferred to be in this sense desirable.", true then?
sidgwick.md:5: argue/readings: S14 is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic and sidgwick.logic.3 have "gap_filled → rational_benevolence"; sidgwick.logic.2 has "established → actual_desire_general" and "gap_filled → rational_benevolence"; they differ when only "Mill's reasoning establishes that the general happiness is desirable", "the gap in Mill's expressed argument is filled" and "some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence is supplied" are true, which the first allows
sidgwick.md:5: argue/answered: S14: answered no, which keeps sidgwick.logic.2, to: Suppose "Mill's reasoning establishes that the general happiness is desirable", "the gap in Mill's expressed argument is filled" and "some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence is supplied" are true and "there is an actual desire for the general happiness" is false. Is S14, "There being therefore no actual desire--so far as this reasoning goes--for the general happiness, the proposition that the general happiness is desirable cannot be in this way established: so that there is a gap in the expressed argument, which can, I think, only be filled by some such proposition as that which I have above tried to exhibit as the intuition of Rational Benevolence.", true then?
sidgwick.logic: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: sidgwick.logic and sidgwick.logic.2 have "actual_desire_general → individual_desire_general ∨ aggregate_desire_general"; sidgwick.logic.3 has "actual_desire_general → individual_desire_general ∨ aggregate_desire_general" and "established → actual_desire_general"; they differ when only "an actual desire for the general happiness exists in some individual", "an actual desire for the general happiness exists in an aggregate of individuals" and "Mill's reasoning establishes that the general happiness is desirable" are true, which the first allows
